From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:39:24 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] hwmon: macsmc: report the M4 Pro GPU sensors'
 powered-down placeholders as no data

On the M4 Pro (T6040) the SMC publishes every GPU temperature key (Tg*)
only while the GPU is powered. When the GPU firmware powers the GPU off
at idle, the next 1 Hz SMC update replaces each key with a placeholder:
-4.0 C on the base keys, 0, 2.2 or 3.9 C on the others. Linux reported
those as temperatures: `sensors` showed a 3.9 C GPU, and the seven GPU
thermal zones fed it to the power allocator as a reading.

Describe the placeholder range in the device tree:
apple,no-data-below-millicelsius on a sensor node makes macsmc-hwmon
return -ENODATA for a reading below it, so userspace sees no reading
instead of a false one. The hwmon thermal bridge turns -ENODATA into
THERMAL_TEMP_INVALID, which the thermal core already treats as "no
valid temperature this time": the zone keeps polling and its last real
temperature, and the update is skipped. Without that, the core would
back off on the error and eventually disable the zone as broken, so it
could not trip when the GPU powered up again.

The 22 Tg* sensors of the T604x MacBook Pros get a 10 C threshold. The
real readings measured on a J616s are 36-75 C, and a powered GPU does
not run below 10 C.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
(cherry picked from commit 97bc174a0e86cf91f7fd718482524ca48a950031)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
